"The word \"Fatir\" mentioned in the first verse of Surah Fatir means to split open and is used in the sense of originating creation. Elmalılı states that this word expresses both the initial creation and the recreation on the Day of Resurrection. Furthermore, it is explained that the angels are messengers and were created with two, three, and four wings. These numbers of wings are not meant for restriction, but to indicate abundance; indeed, Gabriel was seen with six hundred wings. Allah adds to creation as He wills.\n\nIn the second verse, it is emphasized that whatever mercy Allah opens for people, none can withhold, and whatever He withholds, none can release. He is the Almighty and the All-Wise. The third verse calls people to reflect upon Allah's blessings. The fourth verse offers comfort to the Prophet. In the fifth and sixth verses, warnings are given regarding the deceptive nature of worldly life and Satan's deception in the name of Allah. In the seventh verse, it is announced that there is a severe punishment for the disbelievers, and forgiveness and a great reward for the believers.\n\nIn the eighth verse, it is understood that the person whose evil deeds are made appealing to him is not like those who believe and perform righteous deeds. Allah leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. In the ninth verse, the resurrection after death (nushur) is described through the winds lifting the clouds and giving life to a dead land. In the tenth verse, it is stated that whoever desires honor can only attain it through good words that ascend to Allah and the righteous deeds that elevate them.\n\nIn the eleventh verse, it is recounted that man was created from dust, then from a drop of sperm (nutfah), made into pairs, and that everything is recorded in a Book. In the twelfth verse, it is conveyed that the two seas are not equal, and from each—both fresh and salt water—fresh meat and ornaments are extracted. The thirteenth verse demonstrates the sovereignty of Allah's kingdom through the merging of night and day, and the subjugation of the sun and the moon. It is emphasized that the idols cannot manage even something as trivial as the thin membrane of a date stone (\"qitmir\").\n\nIn the eighteenth verse, it is explained that no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another, and that everyone will carry their own responsibility. It is described that even if a heavily laden soul calls out, none of its load will be carried, and close kinship will be of no avail. Only those who fear their Lord in the unseen (ghayb) and establish prayer are purified. In verses nineteen through twenty-two, it is expressed that the believer and the disbeliever are not equal, using the analogies of the blind and the seeing, darkness and light, shade and heat, and the dead and the living. The Prophet is only a warner.\n\nStarting from the twenty-seventh verse, examples are given of Allah sending down water from the sky, producing fruits of diverse colors, and mountains having tracks of various hues. In the twenty-eighth verse, it is stated that humans, beasts, and cattle are likewise of diverse colors, and that only those with knowledge truly fear Allah. In verses twenty-nine and thirty, it is explained that those who recite the Book of Allah, establish prayer, and spend from what they have been provided secretly and openly hope for an unperishing trade, and will receive their rewards in full. In the thirty-second verse, it is stated that the Book was inherited by the chosen servants, and that these servants fall into three classes: those who wrong themselves, those who follow a middle course, and those who are foremost in good deeds. Following this, the blessings of Paradise and the torment of Hell are depicted.\n\nThe diversity of colors in the mountains is proof of Allah's divine power. Among humans, beasts, and cattle, there are likewise those of diverse colors. Reverence and fear of Allah are found only in those who possess knowledge. Scholars are the ones who fear Him the most because they know Allah's majesty and forgiveness.\n\nThose who recite the Book of Allah, establish prayer properly, and spend secretly and openly from the sustenance given to them hope for a trade that will never perish. Allah will grant them their rewards in full and bestow upon them even more from His bounty. He is Forgiving (Ghafur) and Appreciative (Shakur); He pardons sins and does not leave righteous deeds unrewarded.\n\nThe Book revealed to you is the Book that confirms what came before it and distinguishes truth from falsehood. Then We made that Book an inheritance for those whom We chose among Our servants. Among them are some who wrong themselves, some who follow a middle course, and some who take the lead in good deeds. Indeed, being foremost in good deeds is the great favor.\n\nThe chosen servants will enter the Gardens of Eden ('Adn) and will be adorned with bracelets of gold. There, they will be greeted with \"Peace.\" While in the world, Allah had granted them enough of a lifetime to reflect and sent them a warner. They no longer have any excuse.\n\nAllah knows the unseen of the heavens and the earth; He sees what is within the hearts. It is He who made you successors upon the earth. Whoever disbelieves, his disbelief is against himself. Those you associate as partners have created nothing upon the earth. Allah holds the heavens and the earth lest they cease to exist. He is Forbearing and Forgiving.\n\nThe disbelievers had sworn that if a warner came to them, they would surely be rightly guided. But when the prophet came, this only increased their aversion. They acted arrogantly on earth and devised evil plots. Yet evil plots engulf only their own makers. You will find no change in the Sunnah of Allah.\n\nHave they not traveled through the earth and observed how the end was for those before them? They were mightier in strength. There is nothing that can frustrate Allah. If Allah were to immediately seize people for the sins they have committed, He would not leave a single living creature on the face of the earth. But He defers them until an appointed term. When their appointed time arrives, He does what is necessary. Allah is All-Seeing of His servants."